  "lineage": "Tendai-school-and-Shingon-school Japanese Buddhist sutra-chant tradition with characteristic neumed-melisma vocal-line preserving Tang-dynasty Chinese-Buddhist liturgical music transmitted via 8th-9th-century Japanese-monastic-pilgrimage. The two surviving lineages — Tendai-shōmyō (anchored at Mount Hiei monastic complex, established 805 CE by Saichō) and Shingon-shōmyō (anchored at Mount Kōya monastic complex, established 816 CE by Kūkai) — preserve separate but related sutra-and-bonbai canonical-chant-vocabulary repertoires. The chant unfolds in free-rhythm ametrical-pulse, layers single-monk solo-leader against full-monastic-choir responsorial sections, and uses three-pitch-level neumed notation (kotsufu) for melodic contour preservation across centuries.",
